> account does.
> 
> When a user issues the ftp command to a remote system for example like
> 
> % ftp johndoe.anynet.com
> 
> they are presented with the username request, as expected.  Upon
> answering that query the user SHOULD get a password request.  However,
> the ftp program 'blows' through the password request and sends a
> 'null' password to the remote ftp server which correctly responds:
> 
> 530 Login or password incorrect!
> Login failed.
> 
> It is as though the system pressed a RETURN for the user.  A new
> account has this problem.  All old accounts, except root, have this
> problem.  It is not a problem with the remote ftp server.  No other
> system exhibits this problem.
> 
> I strongly suspect it is a file protection issue but for the life of
> me I have not been able to figure out where!  Using another Tru64
> system as a template I believe I have correctly reset all file
> protections, including the s-bits, in the /bin, /usr/..., /sbin/...,
> /dev/..., and /etc/... trees.
> 
> I could use some new ideas.  Any help would be appreciated.  Thank
> you.
> 
> - Nelson ...
It's like you have a .netrc file with the wrong password.  Which version 
are you running?

I would write a script that harvests file modes from the backup dump or 
another system and applies them to the live system.  You could coddle 
something together with vrestore -vt , awk/sed/perl and chmod.  If you 
compile the Linux stat command, you can get numeric modes right off the bat.

Or you could just restore the system to before the boo-boo.  All you 
need is / and /usr and possibly /var.  This is probably the best bet.

Unless the system is restored, you'll be getting these little surprises 
for awhile, and as time progresses, you are less in a position to 
actually do the restore.

Mario




